ID: B3-Ch05a-Intro
TEXT:

"Ah, back at last," the king of the underground says, without looking up from the parchment pinned beneath his forearm.

He stands near the entrance of the rebel redoubt, an oil lamp smoking at his elbow as he marks a courier's dispatch with a blunt charcoal pencil. The subterranean air carries the faint, acrid draft of surface fires and sulfur through the intake shafts. Before I can unhook my crossbow strap, he drops a question that stops the whole line cold.

"I assume negotiations with the Overseer are concluded?"

"I don't know what you mean," I say, keeping my voice flat and my weight balanced over my boots. "I thought our meeting with the machine was set for tomorrow morning."

The king exhales slowly, sets the charcoal down on the rough timber table, and fixes me with a dull, bloodshot stare.

"Listen to me," he says, pressing the heels of his hands into his eyes. "Matters are grim enough on the surface without us wasting breath on theater. I will give it to you plain. I expected you to go behind my back from the moment we first spoke. That is why I had Breynan stick to your flank. An insurance policy, to make sure your private dealings did not end in disaster for this city."

"Breynan," Kate says, her green cloak stiffening as her fingers curl near her belt. "Did you—"

"I didn't say a word, I swear it!" Breynan says quickly, holding his palms out. "I haven't spoken to the king since we left the gate!"

"You misunderstand, Kate," the king says, waving a dismissive hand. "I gave Breynan no orders. He went of his own free will, not as my spy. What tipped my hand was you."

"Me?" Kate asks, her jaw tightening.

"You," the king replies. "From our first exchange, your eyes made your opinion of me entirely clear. That would hardly matter if the healer led your company. But he made it plain that you decide things by consensus, and my instincts told me you would never willingly join hands with my men. So at the end of that council, I forbade Breynan from speaking with the Overseer. I knew precisely how he would take the slight—and that if he saw you heading for the surface, he would follow."

The memory clicks into place: the king glancing toward Kate at the close of our first audience, a heartbeat before telling Breynan he was barred from the summit. The man had read our formation before we even reached the tunnels.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Intro2
TEXT:

"What kind of absurd gamble is that?" Melindra asks, leaning back against a timber post with her arms crossed over her leather vest. "You sidelined the one man who actually understands the Overseer on nothing more than a hunch? What if you guessed wrong? What if Daren talked us into waiting for morning and marching up with your soldiers?"

The king gives a dry, single-shouldered shrug.

"Then I would have told Breynan I changed my mind and sent him along after all," he says, tapping the dispatch pile with a stained knuckle. "Besides, it was hardly a blind guess. Every step you took afterward proved me right. You never set down your packs, even in the quarters we gave you, and the ice mage spent our second council broadcasting her contempt. By then I knew you would seek out the machine on your own. That was why I proposed an alliance of convenience rather than trust—one where either side is free to cut ties the second our interests diverge. You recall the terms, I trust?"

"So you are saying it is your turn to strike from behind?" Illuna asks, standing rigid with her aura clamped down tight to mask the ache in her back.

"Good heavens, no," the king says. "I have no quarrel with you. Breynan may question my judgment, but his loyalty to Ollendor is beyond dispute. If you had done anything to endanger our people, he would not be standing beside you in silence. That is enough for me. Your healer has spent every waking hour in the quarantine ward keeping my men alive. Treating you as enemies would be an insult to his work."

"Does this mean you don't care what we discussed with the Overseer?" Kate asks, her dark eyes unblinking.

"Of course I care," the king says. "I intend to ask. You are simply under no obligation to answer. So—will you share the outcome of your summit, or keep it to yourselves?"

choice(""Of course not."", B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_b3_ch5_negotiations = 0)
choice(""I mean, the cat's out of the bag already, so I guess we might as well tell you."", B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_b3_ch5_negotiations = 1)
choice(""Sure, we'll tell you. For a price."", B3-Ch05a-Price, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Price, v_b3_ch5_negotiations = 2)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Price
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 2 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 7 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 8) {
"Sure, we'll tell you. For a price."

"A price?" The king pauses, his thumb tracing the rim of a small leather pouch at his belt. "To be blunt, I have no intention of emptying my coffers for stale intelligence. Either you struck a clandestine pact with the Overseer—in which case you will lie to my face—or you discussed ways to corner the king, all of which collapsed the moment he locked down the city. Still, I will offer ten gold coins upfront if you confirm or deny my assessment. What do you say?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 2 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 2) {
"Sure, we'll tell you. For a price."

Kate and Breynan both flinch toward me, their shoulders locking before they catch themselves and stare fixedly ahead. The king keeps his eyes on me, unbothered by the twitch in our ranks.

"A price?" The king pauses, his thumb tracing the rim of a small leather pouch at his belt. "To be blunt, I have no intention of emptying my coffers for stale intelligence. Either you struck a clandestine pact with the Overseer—in which case you will lie to my face—or you discussed ways to corner the king, all of which collapsed the moment he locked down the city. Still, I will offer ten gold coins upfront if you confirm or deny my assessment. What do you say?"

}
choice("Confirm that the information from the Overseer has become irrelevant.", B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_b3_ch5_negotiations = 3)
choice("Tell him that ten gold coins is too small a sum to be worth any answer.", B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Contacting, v_b3_ch5_negotiations = 4)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Contacting
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch5_opportunist,1) if (v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 3)
achievement("Opportunist",v_ac_b3_ch5_opportunist)
#if(v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 1 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 7 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 8) {
"I mean, the cat's out of the bag already, so I guess we might as well tell you."

"I am listening," the king says, resting his forearms against the table.

"Not much to tell," I say, shifting the pack off my sore shoulder. "The Overseer pointed out that the king has been slipping out of the palace on regular circuits. We were supposed to shadow his scouts, track his movements, and lay an ambush along his route. But that was before he sealed the outer gates. Whatever timetable he was keeping is useless now."

"As I suspected," the king murmurs, blowing a breath through his mustache. "Even so, I appreciate the candor. Matters are difficult enough without having to second-guess our guests. Get some sleep, all of you. Tomorrow will demand everything we have. We speak again at dawn."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 1 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 1) {
"I mean, the cat's out of the bag already, so I guess we might as well tell you."

Kate and Breynan snap their heads toward me for a fraction of a second, their jaws tightening before they remember to hold still. The king doesn't notice, his attention fixed squarely on me.

"I am listening," the king says, resting his forearms against the table.

"Not much to tell," I say, shifting the pack off my sore shoulder. "The Overseer pointed out that the king has been slipping out of the palace on regular circuits. We were supposed to shadow his scouts, track his movements, and lay an ambush along his route. But that was before he sealed the outer gates. Whatever timetable he was keeping is useless now."

"As I suspected," the king murmurs, blowing a breath through his mustache. "Even so, I appreciate the candor. Matters are difficult enough without having to second-guess our guests. Get some sleep, all of you. Tomorrow will demand everything we have. We speak again at dawn."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 0) {
"Of course not."

The king shrugs, picking up his charcoal pencil once more.

"Suit yourself," he says. "It is not as though I cannot piece together the broad strokes on my own. Go get some rest. Tomorrow will be grueling enough. We speak in the morning."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 3) {
"We have a deal," I say. "You guessed right. The Overseer's intelligence is obsolete. I could give you the particulars, but since you only paid for a confirmation, I assume you don't need them."

"Correct," the king says. "I have no use for expired plans. But I appreciate the honesty. Here is your coin."

He counts ten heavy gold pieces into my palm. The metal is cold and nicked from circulation. I drop them into my belt pouch with a satisfying clink.

"Now that business is settled," the king says, "go get some rest. Tomorrow will be grueling enough. We speak in the morning."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_negotiations == 4) {
"Ten gold coins isn't worth the breath it takes to answer," I say. "Keep your money."

The king shrugs, returning the pouch to his belt.

"As you like," he says. "I see little point in turning down free coin, but I will not waste time haggling. Go get some rest. Tomorrow will be grueling enough. We speak in the morning."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations != 0 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations != 4 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations != 0 && v_b3_ch5_negotiations != 4) {
"Is there anything you need of me, Sire?" Breynan asks, stepping toward the table.

"You can start by taking half these reports," the king says, gesturing toward an aide holding a thick bundle of parchment. "Take from the bottom. Those should be clear enough even without an intimate knowledge of the city grid. We must relocate as many civilians as possible before sunrise, so prepare for a long night. We will be here until dawn."

"Understood," Breynan says, drawing a sheet from the pile.

We leave them to their work and turn down the tunnel toward our quarters. The waiting hall is crowded with displaced families, rebel runners steering weeping mothers and children toward whatever empty corner remains. Space is clearly running out; several escorts are already guiding refugees toward the mess hall instead of the barracks.

"For a second back there, I thought you were about to mention our other arrangement with the Overseer," Kate whispers once we are past the sentry post.

"Give me some credit," I mutter back, keeping my voice low against the rumble of the crowd. "I'm not that reckless."

}
