#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"Should we contact Daren and Hadrik?" Leila writes, hovering glowing blue script in the air as we reach the corridor arch. "They may have been trying our transceivers while we were away."

"Good idea," I say, pulling the brass device from my pocket. "I'll raise them now."

I turn the small dials and call Daren first. I give him a quick, stripped-down summary—leaving out the energy-harvesting machinery beneath the seredium tower—and assure him we made it back in one piece. He's been trying to reach us for over an hour between wards. Once he agrees to meet us outside the infirmary, I dial Hadrik and repeat the brief account. The dwarf promises to meet us in ten minutes and cuts the connection with a click.

The transceiver is barely back in my coat when Daren steps out of the quarantine tunnel, holding Arraka's gold amulet right against his chin like a speaking horn while shouting into it. Kelrim trails a few paces behind in his stained apothecary apron, padding along in silence as they head our way.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"Should we contact Daren and Hadrik?" Leila writes, hovering glowing blue script in the air as we reach the corridor arch. "They may have been trying our transceivers while we were underground."

"Good idea," I say, pulling the brass device from my pocket. "And Rose too. I told her I'd call the moment we got back to base."

I turn the small dials and call Daren first. I give him a quick, stripped-down summary—leaving out the energy-harvesting machinery beneath the seredium tower—and assure him we made it back in one piece. He's been trying to reach us for over an hour between wards. Once he agrees to meet us outside the infirmary, I dial Hadrik and repeat the brief account. The dwarf promises to meet us in ten minutes and cuts the connection with a click.

The transceiver is barely back in my coat when Daren steps out of the quarantine tunnel, holding Arraka's gold amulet right against his chin like a speaking horn while shouting into it. Kelrim trails a few paces behind in his stained apothecary apron, padding along in silence as they head our way.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Untangle, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Untangle)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Untangle
TEXT:

"If you had explained it like that from the start, we wouldn't have wasted half the day on aura drills!" Daren shouts, holding the heavy gold amulet level with his jaw as he stomps down the passage.

"I did explain it, you thickheaded oaf," Arraka snarls from the gold casing, her voice metallic and harsh. "I have told you every day since we crossed the border: untangle the currents with your mind. What part of that requires a translator?"

"The part where your instructions sound like gibberish!" Daren snaps, wiping sweat from his forehead with the back of his steel gauntlet. "How was I supposed to guess you meant actual knots? I was trying to redirect every stream one by one! You couldn't explain water to a drowning man!"

"Is that so?" Arraka sneers. "Then why did the goblin grasp it on the first try? One sentence, and he had it. What is your excuse, hero?"

"Maybe you understand each other because you're both monsters!"

"I am an elf, you miserable clod! How many times do I have to say it?"

"Should I take it you made progress on the quarantine cure?" Illuna asks as they reach our group, her face settling into its usual dry mask.

#if(False) {
"So, what you are saying is that it is now your turn to backstab us?" Illuna asks the king.

"Good heavens, no!" the king of the underground says. "I'm not planning to do anything to you. Breynan might not be all that loyal to me, but he is most certainly loyal to the people of Ollendor, so I couldn't possibly imagine him still being on friendly terms with you if you had tried to hurt our people in any way. Given that he's not saying anything, I'm going to assume that at the very least you're not working against the interests of our people. That is good enough for me. After all, your healer friend has been working tirelessly to save my men's lives in the quarantine zone ever since he came here, so it would be an insult to him if I were to treat any of you as my enemies."

"Does this mean that you're not going to ask us what we discussed with the Overseer?" Kate says.

"Obviously I'm still going to ask you that question," the king says. "But you are free to not answer me if that is your decision. So... are you going to tell me what were the results of your negotiations with the Overseer or not?"

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"Progress, yes," Arraka says. "Only because the little green one translated my words into something simple enough for a primate to digest. Daren finally understands what is rotting their cores. Now he just needs the reagents."

"Reagents we don't have," Daren says, lowering the amulet with a heavy sigh. "The tools are simple enough, but Kelrim says the city's apothecaries are nearly picked clean. Rogue settlements don't see much caravan trade, and the herbs we need don't grow in these plains. We will have to scour half the market stalls in Ollendor—which would be difficult even if we weren't wanted fugitives under curfew. If the guards are enforcing the lockdown, half those shops won't even unbolt their shutters."

"You could ask Rose," Kate suggests, glancing at the transceiver in my hand. "She knows the local flora. She might know common substitutes that ordinary herb sellers keep on hand, so you don't have to turn the whole market inside out."

Daren nods, rubbing his chin where the helmet strap chafed. "Good thought. I'll ask her later tonight."

"If you are going into the markets, I am coming with you," Kelrim says, stepping forward and adjusting the strap of his satchel. "I buy my own supplies there. I know which back-alley vendors are likely to have stock left."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"Progress, yes," Arraka says. "Only because the little green one translated my words into something simple enough for a primate to digest. Daren finally understands what is rotting their cores. Now he just needs the reagents."

"Reagents we don't have," Daren says, lowering the amulet with a heavy sigh. "The tools are simple enough, but Kelrim says the city's apothecaries are nearly picked clean. Rogue settlements don't see much caravan trade, and the herbs we need don't grow in these plains. We will have to scour half the market stalls in Ollendor—which would be difficult even if we weren't wanted fugitives under curfew. If the guards are enforcing the lockdown, half those shops won't even unbolt their shutters."

"If you are going into the markets, I am coming with you," Kelrim says, stepping forward and adjusting the strap of his satchel. "I buy my own supplies there. I know which back-alley vendors are likely to have stock left."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 1) {
"Are you sure, Kelrim?" Daren asks gently, resting a gauntleted palm on the goblin's shoulder. "The contagion moves far slower in goblin blood. You could wait a week or two for the curfew to lift before risking the streets. There is no need to put yourself in front of royal guards."

"And let the humans in those beds die while you search alone?" Kelrim's ears pin back against his scalp. "No more innocent blood on this city's stones. Not while I can still walk. If I have breath in my lungs, I help."

The phrase catches in my throat. Innocent blood. He doesn't mean it as an accusation, but the memory of the goblin sentries we cut down outside the perimeter hits like cold water. The fact that he holds no grudge only makes the silence heavier. A glance at Daren and Kate shows the same tightening around their jaws. Daren swallows whatever protest he has left.

"Then I'll watch your back," Daren says quietly. "I give you my word, Kelrim. Nothing touches you on the surface."

"Thank you," Kelrim says, bowing his head slightly. "If blood is drawn, my magic will be ready."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins != 1) {
"Are you sure, Kelrim?" Daren asks, looking down at him. "The contagion moves far slower in goblin blood. You could wait a week or two for the curfew to lift before risking the streets. There is no need to put yourself in front of royal guards."

"And let the humans in those beds die while you search alone?" Kelrim's ears pin back against his scalp. "No more innocent blood on this city's stones. Not while I can still walk. If I have breath in my lungs, I help."

"If your mind is made up, I won't argue," Daren says. "Just stay behind my shield when we hit the surface. You never know what's waiting in the alleys."

"I will be careful," Kelrim says, bowing his head slightly. "Thank you. If blood is drawn, my magic will be ready."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Adventure, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Adventure)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Adventure
TEXT:

"Let's move this to our quarters," I say. "Too many ears in the open corridor. Hadrik was heading this way anyway—we should cross paths on the road."

"Right," Daren says. "Until tomorrow, Kelrim."

"Rest well," Kelrim says, bowing his head as we turn down the branch.

"Petal— Illuna, I mean," Flower pipes up, her voice small and muffled as she rubs at her eyes. "Can I sleep a little longer? My head still feels heavy..."

"Go ahead," Illuna says, her expression softening for a bare fraction of a second before hardening again.

"Wake me if something happens," Flower murmurs, letting her chin drop against her chest.

Leila's fingers trace glowing blue glyphs across the dim air between us. "Do you think she is shaken by what was beneath the tower?"

"Hard to look at that harvesting rig and not be," I murmur back, keeping my voice beneath Daren's hearing. "Though she was already half-asleep when Illuna pulled her up. Could just be—"

"Well, look who decided to show up!"

Hadrik's booming voice echoes down the stone passage, his broad dwarf grin preceding him as he rounds the bend with an axe slung across his leather-clad shoulder. "Off playing hero without me again? Keeping all the glory to yourselves, are you?"

"Glory isn't the word I'd use," I say. "We'll lay it out in the room. Half the city is packing through these halls."

"Quiet as a tomb back where we're sleeping," Hadrik says, falling into step beside us, his heavy boots thudding against the stone. "Though I suppose that's by design. The king tucked us into the farthest hole in the rock. Not keen on us fraternizing with the locals, I wager."

"Suits us fine," Daren says. "Do we bear right here?"

"First right, straight along the seam, then third left," I say.

"Lead on, then," Daren says.

As we reach the turning, Albert appears from the side tunnel, herding a cluster of frightened refugees toward a side hall with a grim, exhausted scowl. His eyes meet mine for a fraction of a second before he cuts his gaze away, staring straight ahead as though the passage were empty.

choice("Call out to him and wish him a good evening.", B3-Ch05a-Evening, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Evening)
choice("Don't say anything to him.", B3-Ch05a-Anything, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Anything)
choice("Mock him for having been reduced to escort duty.", B3-Ch05a-Mock, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Mock)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Evening
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"Good evening, Albert!" I call out. "Pleasant night for a stroll!"

Albert narrows his eyes, gives a tight, reluctant nod, and keeps walking without breaking his rigid stride. We follow Hadrik past the archway and into the deeper tunnels where the stone turns damp and cold.

The moment the heavy timber door thuds shut behind us, Daren sets his shield down and tends to Illuna. For all her iron composure, the dark cloth of her tunic is soaked through with blood from where the lessathi assassin's dagger punched into her flank. Daren kneels in his scuffed plate, his gauntlets off, hands bathing the wound in a soft, steady white glow. Muscle and skin knit back together under the light while Illuna keeps her breathing measured and her eyes blank.

Once the flesh seals and Daren wipes his palms, we wave him and Hadrik toward the rough bench. When we finish laying out what we found beneath the seredium tower—the chained men, the siphon conduits, the energy draining into the city grid—the silence in the room splits three ways. Daren's jaw sets with grim, white-knuckled fury; Hadrik's face darkens with pure rage; and Arraka's amulet rests on the wooden table in mocking indifference.

"Those absolute bastards!" Hadrik roars, slamming a thick fist into his palm with a crack that rattles the lanterns. "Where is this cursed tower? I will tear the machine off its mountings with my bare hands!"

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"Good evening, Albert!" I call out. "Pleasant night for a stroll!"

Albert narrows his eyes, gives a tight, reluctant nod, and keeps walking without breaking his rigid stride. We follow Hadrik past the archway and into the deeper tunnels where the stone turns damp and cold.

The moment the heavy timber door thuds shut behind us, we wave Daren and Hadrik toward the rough bench. I pull the brass transceiver from my coat and dial Rose so she can hear the debrief alongside the rest of us.

When we finish laying out what we found beneath the seredium tower—the chained men, the siphon conduits, the energy draining into the city grid—the silence in the room splits four ways. Daren's jaw sets with grim, white-knuckled fury; Hadrik's face darkens with pure rage; Rose stays utterly silent over the hiss of the line; and Arraka's amulet rests on the wooden table in mocking indifference.

"Those absolute bastards!" Hadrik roars, slamming a thick fist into his palm with a crack that rattles the lanterns. "Where is this cursed tower? I will tear the machine off its mountings with my bare hands!"

}
"You can't smash the machine," Melindra says flatly, leaning her shoulder against the doorframe. "It has to be powered down from the control station. Weren't you listening?"

"Then I'll break every skull in the tower until someone points me to the levers!" Hadrik snaps, turning on her. "Someone in there knows where it sits!"

"You won't break a thing, you lumbering clown," Arraka cackles from the amulet. "You'll be lucky to land one blow before the continent's ward catches your knuckles and hurls you back across the ocean."

Hadrik growls, pacing the flagstones like a caged bear. "Then I'll dodge their guards and punch through every wall until I find the core! We cannot sit here! Why aren't the rest of you furious?"

"Is that a rhetorical question, or do you want a list?" Arraka asks.

"Calm down, Hadrik," Daren says, holding up a steady hand. "I am just as sickened as you are. But charging blind won't save them. Even if we breached that basement, there are three other towers in Ollendor doing the exact same thing. Settle one, and the entire royal garrison comes down on our heads. We cannot fight an entire army alone. If we want those people out, the throne has to fall. And to do that, we need allies. What was your read on the Overseer? Will it cooperate?"

#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 9) {
"Doubtful," I say, leaning back against the cold stone. "Billy Bob will probably pull its core the moment he comes around for selling him out. I doubt we'll see it again."

"To the abyss with the machine!" Hadrik says. "It's iron and wire. The rebels have the steel. Can we trust them to mount a raid?"

"The rebels won't touch the towers," Kate says, her arms folded tightly across her cloak. "Their king made that clear. He won't spend a single soldier on a rescue, even knowing innocent people are strapped to the drain. The only thing an alliance gets us is steel against the palace on the surface. Nothing more."

"Here is a novel thought," Arraka sneers. "Stop begging help from every pathetic mortal in a ten-mile radius and solve your own messes. Do you think these gutter rebels will hold your hand when you face the God of Time? Will they stand beside you when my five-thousand-year-old self reaches down your throats and pulls your spines out through your teeth? When will you mongrels understand that this is not a game? Take your drills seriously, or die like the amateurs you are."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy <= 6 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 0) {
"Hard to say," I answer, leaning back against the cold stone. "It seemed grateful enough that we took care of Billy Bob, and willing to trade favors. But its motives are its own. I wouldn't turn my back on it."

"To the abyss with the machine!" Hadrik says. "It's iron and wire. The rebels have the steel. Can we trust them to mount a raid?"

"The rebels won't touch the towers," Kate says, her arms folded tightly across her cloak. "Their king made that clear. He won't spend a single soldier on a rescue, even knowing innocent people are strapped to the drain. The only thing an alliance gets us is steel against the palace on the surface. Nothing more."

"Here is a novel thought," Arraka sneers. "Stop begging help from every pathetic mortal in a ten-mile radius and solve your own messes. Do you think these gutter rebels will hold your hand when you face the God of Time? Will they stand beside you when my five-thousand-year-old self reaches down your throats and pulls your spines out through your teeth? When will you mongrels understand that this is not a game? Take your drills seriously, or die like the amateurs you are."

}
