ID: B3-Ch05a-Unfreeze
TEXT:

"Why don't you tell Kate how to unfreeze people if you're so smart?"

"Because I am not an elementalist!" Arraka barks. "Explaining how I twist magical fields won't help her in the slightest. Elementalists channel through instinct and feeling, not rigid equations. She needs someone who speaks that language, otherwise it is like trying to teach Daren—a complete waste of breath against a brick wall."

"Will mastering the frost help against the God of Time?" I ask.

"Not directly," Arraka says. "But it breaks Kate out of her predictable habits. She cannot rely on long-range artillery forever. Flower can supply fresh concepts—the brat has an annoying knack for improvisation when she isn't terrified. Now, the captain of the royal guard. That old butcher is a more immediate problem. Ideally I would drill all of you in countering his aura compression, but we don't have the hours. We may run into him again tomorrow. So the solution is simple: when he steps into the street, leave him to Daren."

"Me?" Daren blinks, startled.

"Yes, you!" Arraka snaps. "You finally grasped how to untangle aura flows, didn't you? When he tries to drown you in his aura and pin you to the pavement, you unravel the weave. You slip right through the bind. It will be considerably harder against a seasoned killer than against dying patients, but that enchanted blade of yours cuts magical conduits. Cut the knots with the sword. You understand the principle, don't you?"

Daren looks down at his sword pommel, drawing an inch of enchanted steel to check the edge before clicking it home. "Yes... I believe I do."

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"Good," Arraka says. "That is enough lecturing for one night. Go to sleep."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"Good," Arraka says. "That is enough lecturing for one night. Hmm... though while we are on the subject—Rose, are you still on the line?"

Silence answers from the transceiver. Arraka raises her volume until the brass casing vibrates against the wood.

"ROSE! Wake up!"

A shuffle of footsteps echoes over the speaker, followed by Rose's breathless voice.

"I'm here!" she says. "Suzie woke up with a nightmare, so I took her some sweet biscuits from the kitchen. Did I miss anything?"

"Nothing vital," Arraka says. "Did Barry explain what you must do before the next ritual?"

"Yes," Rose says hesitantly. "I have to prevent the God of Time from siphoning my power again. I've been practicing, but..."

"You don't know if the ward holds?"

"I think I understand the shape of it," Rose murmurs. "It's just difficult to know if it works without an enemy to test it against."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Lockdown, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Lockdown) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Reverse, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Reverse) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Reverse
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 0) {
"Do not fret over it," Arraka says. "Grasping the intent is enough for now. Once the foundation settles, I will give you a proper method to test your wards. But the real trial arrives at the next ritual. By then you must be sharp enough to sever Selkram's first siphon on your reversal magic without alerting him. He will stumble, but he will write it off as a tremor in the link. Once you know your defense holds, drop the ward and let him draw the remainder. We cannot fight him yet; we preserve the trap until the third ceremony. By then, with luck, this rabble will be marginally less useless."

"Is... that all you need of me?" Rose asks softly.

"No," Arraka says. "I want clarity on your progress. I hear you caught a glimpse of the future and managed to hold time still long enough for these idiots to bypass a hall of traps. Can you decelerate time yet? What about rewinding it?"

"Oh, heavens no," Rose says, her voice trembling slightly against the receiver. "I cannot slow time, let alone reverse it. I only attempted the freeze because the mechanics seemed the most straightforward in my memory. Slowing time is far beyond me. As for rewinding... I couldn't command that consciously even while anchored to the Magium. I still don't know how it triggered when you breached the amulet. And the premonition arrived without warning. I did nothing to summon it."

"Premonitions are simply your senses adapting to temporal currents," Arraka says. "You will never trigger them on command. Normally they warn of threats to your own hide, not strangers. That your first instinct was shielding Illuna suggests you spend entirely too much energy fretting over others. Still—keep drilling. If you maintain this pace, you may prove more lethal than every warrior in this room. A god flails like a child when the seconds stop moving around him. Delicious irony for a deity of time."

"I don't know if I can hold him," Rose murmurs. "Holding thirty seconds today nearly made me faint."

"On the earthen plane, yes," Arraka says. "During the ritual you will be tethered to the Magium, with vast reservoirs to draw upon. Keep practicing. And do not neglect the deflection drills."

"I will," Rose says. "Thank you, Arraka."

"Good. Class is over. Go to sleep."

}
