choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Guilty, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Guilty) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Volume
TEXT:

"Barry, could you drop the volume and hold the receiver to your ear?" Rose whispers over the channel. "Just for a moment."

"Done," I say, dialing down the brass knob and pressing the cold rim against my ear. "Go ahead."

"I know Flower and Illuna have been at each other's throats since yesterday," Rose says gently. "Did you volunteer for this watch with Illuna to help them make peace?"

I glance toward Illuna. She sits with her back against the damp cavern wall, completely unbothered, her eyes fixed on the dark corridor ahead.

"Pretty much," I say.

"I knew it," Rose says with a quiet breath. "Mind if I stay on the line for a bit? I want to talk to her too, and she might actually listen if we approach her together."

"Be my guest," I say, and Illuna's head snaps toward me at the change in tone. "Just like old times."

"Wonderful," Rose says. "Give me two minutes. I promised Suzie a plate of honey biscuits before bed."

"Am I to understand Rose is joining our sentry duty?" Illuna asks as soon as I lower the device and restore the volume dial.

"Only for a little while," I say, settling onto the stone floor across from her.

"Splendid," Illuna says dryly. "Perhaps this time I will actually manage to eat."

"Don't hold your breath," I tell her.

We settle at the first corridor junction—far enough from the heavy timber door that our voices won't carry to the sleeping pallets, but positioned to watch the main approach. We barely take our seats before Melindra strolls past the archway, hands tucked lazily into her pockets.

"Don't mind me!" she calls out breezily, not even glancing our way. "Just stretching my legs before turning in. Pretend I'm not here!"

She gives an airy wave and vanishes down the left passage into the dark.

"I'm back," Rose announces through the brass speaker a minute later.

At the sound of Rose's voice, Illuna weaves her fingers with a flick of her wrist. A fresh sandwich materializes in her palm out of thin air, and she takes a slow, deliberate bite.

"You really conjured food," I say.

"If we are staging a faithful reenactment of our past vigils, we should observe proper custom," Illuna replies, chewing calmly.

"Right," I say, glancing around at the damp bedrock. "So... fascinating lichens on these walls, aren't they? Wonder how long they've been growing."

Illuna stares at me over the crust. "Ye gods. You aren't actually using the same opening lines, are you? The reenactment was a jest. You don't have to recite old scripts."

"I changed it!" I say defensively. "Last time I brought up the weather."

"Scintillating," Illuna says. "Well, if subterranean moss is your passion, Rose is an herbalist. She will gladly indulge you while I finish my meal."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Guilty, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Guilty)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Guilty
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"Actually, there was something else Barry and I wanted to discuss," Rose says gently over the speaker.

"Naturally," Illuna sighs, taking another measured bite.

"The real reason I asked for this shift," I say, resting my elbows on my knees, "is that we need to finish the conversation about you and Flower."

Illuna rolls her eyes toward the cavern roof. "Not this again. We settled that argument."

"We didn't settle anything," I say. "Flower wants you to acknowledge her as an adult. That's why she's putting on that stiff, serious act and calling you Illuna instead of Petal. She's desperate for you to recognize that twenty-nine years have passed. Why fight her on it? Why does it matter so much if she calls herself grown?"

"Because she isn't grown!" Illuna snaps, her voice sharpening. "None of you treat her as an adult either. You've all called her a nine-year-old to her face!"

"That's teasing," I say. "You've lived in the same skin for twenty years and you're still bickering over it. Aren't you taking it a bit too far?"

Illuna lowers the sandwich to her lap, her blue eyes fixing me with an icy, unforgiving stare. "Let me understand you. You claim the rest of the company views Flower as a full-fledged woman of twenty-nine, and I am the sole person who sees the mind of a child? Rose, do you truly consider Flower an adult?"

"Well..." Rose hesitates over the line. "It's difficult to treat her as an adult when she behaves so much like my younger siblings. But it also feels cruel to dismiss her when being taken seriously means the world to her."

"Look," I say. "Nobody is saying her mental development progressed past childhood. She's nine at heart, but she can still be brave and reliable when the knives come out. None of us expect her to turn into Kate overnight, but would it kill you to meet her halfway? Let her claim twenty-nine if it brings her peace. Where is the harm?"

"The harm is in the lie!" Illuna's voice rises, raw and fierce against the quiet tunnel. "Allowing her to hide inside a fantasy will only destroy her when the delusion breaks! The brutal truth is that she will never grow up. She lost that future the instant I invaded her body twenty years ago, and nothing in this world can undo what I broke. The only mercy I have left is keeping her tethered to reality before she drowns in make-believe!"

I stare at her, the words hanging heavy between us. "Wait a second... Is that what this is about? You're drowning in guilt?"

Illuna glares back, her jaw clenched tight. "And what if I am?"

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
We take our seats on the cold stone floor at the corridor junction—far enough from the timber door to keep our voices from carrying to the sleeping quarters, but positioned to watch the main approach. We barely sit down before Melindra strolls past the archway, hands tucked lazily into her pockets.

"Don't mind me!" she calls out breezily, not even glancing our way. "Just stretching my legs before turning in. Pretend I'm not here!"

She gives an airy wave and vanishes down the left passage into the shadows.

"Been a while since we shared a quiet watch," I say after her footsteps fade into the dark.

"It has," Illuna replies evenly, gazing down the tunnel.

"We ought to do it more often," I say. "Builds camaraderie. Solidifies team bonds."

Illuna raises an eyebrow, her gaze flat. "Do you intend to reach a point tonight, or should I conjure a book while you drone into the void?"

"All right, all right," I say, leaning forward. "The real reason I volunteered is that we need to finish the conversation about you and Flower."

Illuna sighs, closing her eyes for a second. "Not this again. We settled that argument."

"We didn't settle anything," I say. "Flower wants you to acknowledge her as an adult. That's why she's putting on that stiff, serious act and calling you Illuna instead of Petal. She's desperate for you to recognize that twenty-nine years have passed. Why fight her on it? Why does it matter so much if she calls herself grown?"

"Because she isn't grown!" Illuna snaps, turning on me. "None of you treat her as an adult either. You've all called her a nine-year-old to her face!"

"That's teasing," I say. "You've lived in the same skin for twenty years and you're still bickering over it. Aren't you taking it a bit too far? Obviously her mental development froze at nine, but she can still be brave and dependable when the knives come out. Nobody expects her to become Kate, but would it kill you to meet her halfway? Let her claim twenty-nine if it brings her peace. Where is the harm?"

"The harm is in the lie!" Illuna's voice rises, raw and fierce against the quiet tunnel. "Allowing her to hide inside a fantasy will only destroy her when the delusion breaks! The brutal truth is that she will never grow up. She lost that future the instant I invaded her body twenty years ago, and nothing in this world can undo what I broke. The only mercy I have left is keeping her tethered to reality before she drowns in make-believe!"

I stare at her, the words hanging heavy between us. "Wait a second... Is that what this is about? You're drowning in guilt?"

Illuna glares back, her jaw clenched tight. "And what if I am?"

}
choice(""Well, then stop feeling guilty!"", B3-Ch05a-Oblivious, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Oblivious, v_b3_ch5_guilty = 0)
choice(""It doesn't seem to me like Flower is blaming you for what happened."", B3-Ch05a-Oblivious, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Oblivious, v_b3_ch5_guilty = 1)
choice(""Well, to me it kind of feels like you are letting your guilt cloud your judgment."", B3-Ch05a-Oblivious, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Oblivious, v_b3_ch5_guilty = 2)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Oblivious
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch5_guilty == 0) {
"Well, then stop feeling guilty!"

Illuna's mouth twitches into a dry, humorless smirk. "Your bedside manner is truly awe-inspiring."

"What's done is done," I say, spreading my hands. "You can't let a twenty-year-old mistake drag you into the dirt every day of your life. Isn't it time to move past it?"

"Guilt or no guilt, I am protecting her from delusion," Illuna says flatly. "There is nothing to move past."

"You sell her short," I tell her. "She acts like a child, but she isn't stupid. She knows exactly what happened to her body. She simply chooses joy. She finds whatever brightness is available and holds onto it. Given who she is, wouldn't it make more sense to meet her there? Maybe she wishes she could have grown like other girls, but she also loves being young. Why not treat her as a twenty-nine-year-old kid? Can't you grant her that much?"

Illuna looks away into the dark tunnel, silent for a long moment as she considers the words. "I will... consider it."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_guilty == 1) {
"It doesn't seem to me like Flower is blaming you for what happened."

"Of course she doesn't," Illuna says, her voice quiet. "Because she refuses to acknowledge the reality of what our binding cost her. It is easy to forgive a crime you pretend never occurred."

"You sell her short," I tell her. "She acts like a child, but she isn't stupid. She knows exactly what happened to her body. She simply chooses joy. She finds whatever brightness is available and holds onto it. Given who she is, wouldn't it make more sense to meet her there? Maybe she wishes she could have grown like other girls, but she also loves being young. Why not treat her as a twenty-nine-year-old kid? Can't you grant her that much?"

Illuna looks away into the dark tunnel, silent for a long moment as she considers the words. "I will... consider it."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_guilty == 2) {
"Well, to me it kind of feels like you are letting your guilt cloud your judgment."

"I disagree," Illuna says coldly. "Guilt may drive the resolve, but my duty to her remains unchanged. I refuse to let her build her life on a lie."

"You sell her short," I tell her. "She acts like a child, but she isn't stupid. She knows exactly what happened to her body. She simply chooses joy. She finds whatever brightness is available and holds onto it. Given who she is, wouldn't it make more sense to meet her there? Maybe she wishes she could have grown like other girls, but she also loves being young. Why not treat her as a twenty-nine-year-old kid? Can't you grant her that much?"

Illuna looks away into the dark tunnel, silent for a long moment as she considers the words. "I will... consider it."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"If it helps," Rose adds through the transceiver, "I agree with Barry. Flower isn't blind. She doesn't hide from truth; she just embraces what she has. I think you might be worrying too much, Illuna."

"Perhaps," Illuna murmurs, resting her chin on her hand. "I will not rule it out."

"See?" I grin. "Actual progress."

"Does this conclude your grand crusade?" Illuna asks. "Can I retire in peace?"

"Not quite," I say. "I wanted to speak with Flower as well. She said she was only resting for an hour or two, so I figured I'd catch her before our watch ended."

"Then I have an elegant solution," Illuna says. "It has been well over an hour. I will wake her and leave you to your council while I take my sleep. Good night."

"Wait!" I say as the bright blue radiance in her eyes dims. "I thought the four of us could—and you're already gone."

Rose lets out a quiet laugh over the speaker.

"I had forgotten how much I missed her stubbornness," Rose says softly. "I hope they find their way through this."

"Me too," I say.

Faint rustling carries over the line from Thilias, followed by Suzie's muffled voice calling in the distance.

"I'm coming, Suzie!" Rose calls out, then lowers her voice to the receiver. "Barry, I have to go. Suzie can't sleep without a story. Is it all right if I leave you here?"

"Go on," I say. "Take care of her."

"Thank you," Rose says. "Good luck with Flower."

The connection clicks shut. Across from me on the stone, the small body remains motionless, waiting for the switch to complete.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
