"Does this conclude your grand crusade?" Illuna asks. "Can I retire in peace?"

"Not quite," I say. "I wanted to speak with Flower as well. She said she was only resting for an hour or two, so I figured I'd catch her before our watch ended."

"Then I have an elegant solution," Illuna says. "It has been well over an hour. I will wake her and leave you to your council while I take my sleep. Good night."

"Wait!" I say as the bright blue radiance in her eyes dims. "I thought the three of us could—and you're already gone."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Sleeve, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Sleeve)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Sleeve
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch5_days,1) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)
achievement("The good old days",v_ac_b3_ch5_days)
While Illuna hands control over within their shared mind, the body sits cross-legged on the stone, eyes staring blankly into the shadows. When the focus returns, the irises shift to bright emerald green, darting across the corridor in frantic confusion. Illuna clearly dumped her into the physical plane without an explanation.

"Umm..." Flower says, blinking up at me in the dim torchlight. "Illuna said you wanted to talk to me?"

"Right. Yes. I wanted to talk about..."

The sentence dies in my throat.

"About the..." I try again, and hit a complete blank wall.

I have no plan for this. I rarely talk to Flower alone, and bringing up something this delicate out of nowhere feels like walking into a trap with no disarm kit. Do I ease into it with small talk? Would she even open up to me the way Illuna did? How am I supposed to fix a twenty-year rift when I can't even assemble a sentence?

"About..." I squeak.

"Barry, are you all right?" Flower asks, leaning forward with wide, concerned green eyes. "You're sweating a lot."

"Fine! Totally fine," I say, dragging my sleeve across my dripping forehead. "Just... the weather. Very seasonal for this time of year underground, wouldn't you say?"

"Oh, for heaven's sake!" Melindra's voice yells from the side tunnel.

Flower jumps. "Melindra?"

"Melindra?" I repeat, gaping as she marches out from the shadows with a disgusted shake of her head. "You were lurking there the entire time? I didn't feel a ripple of aura!"

"Of course you didn't," Melindra says, tossing her ponytail over her shoulder. "I suppressed it completely, and stillwater signatures are nearly impossible to track as it is. You'd need senses twice as sharp as yours to catch me."

"What were you doing spying on us?" I demand. "You said you were taking a walk!"

"I didn't take a walk," Melindra says, crossing her arms. "I knew something was cooking when you volunteered for sentry duty out of nowhere. I came to watch the show! I had no intention of interfering until you started choking on your own tongue. What is your problem? You trade insults with Illuna like an old merchant, but with Flower you turn to jelly?"

"Illuna doesn't break if I say something clumsy," I mutter, rubbing the back of my neck. "Flower feels... delicate. I don't know what will hurt her."

"She isn't made of glass!" Melindra snaps. "Stop treating her like an infant. Watch—this is how you open a conversation. Flower... Barry was trying to ask about your fight with Illuna."

Flower gasps, clutching her knees. "What? I didn't fight with Illuna!"

choice(""Is this why you just called her Illuna instead of Petal?"", B3-Ch05a-Behavior, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Behavior, v_b3_ch5_demeanor = 0)
choice(""Then why are you still imitating Kate's demeanor instead of acting like yourself?"", B3-Ch05a-Behavior, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Behavior, v_b3_ch5_demeanor = 1)
choice(""Yes, you did. I've seen you having arguments with her multiple times."", B3-Ch05a-Behavior, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Behavior, v_b3_ch5_demeanor = 2)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Behavior
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch5_demeanor == 0) {
"Is this why you just called her Illuna instead of Petal?"

"I don't know what you mean," Flower says, her voice suddenly stiff and flat, copying Kate's clipped delivery. "'Petal' was an infantile nickname from when we first fused. I have outgrown such childish habits."

"Adopting a severe tone doesn't make you mature, you know," I say.

"Yes, it does!" Flower insists, puffing out her cheeks. "I conduct myself with maturity, which makes me an adult. End of discussion."

Melindra tilts her head, studying her. "Flower... why do you want to be an adult so badly? Why does it matter?"

"Because I AM an adult!" Flower says. "What grown woman wants to be treated like a child?"

"Hold that thought," Melindra says.

She unslings her leather satchel, reaches deep inside, and pulls out several smooth stones, arranging them in a neat line on the dust between us.

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_demeanor == 1) {
"Then why are you still imitating Kate's demeanor instead of acting like yourself?"

"I am not imitating Kate," Flower says, making an obvious effort to relax her posture while keeping her tone carefully measured. "I simply discarded childish mannerisms. I behave like an adult now. I see nothing wrong with that."

"Adopting a severe tone doesn't make you mature, you know," I say.

"Yes, it does!" Flower insists, puffing out her cheeks. "I conduct myself with maturity, which makes me an adult. End of discussion."

Melindra tilts her head, studying her. "Flower... why do you want to be an adult so badly? Why does it matter?"

"Because I AM an adult!" Flower says. "What grown woman wants to be treated like a child?"

"Hold that thought," Melindra says.

She unslings her leather satchel, reaches deep inside, and pulls out several smooth stones, arranging them in a neat line on the dust between us.

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_demeanor == 2) {
"Yes, you did. I've seen you having arguments with her multiple times."

"When did we fight?" Flower asks, slipping right back into Kate's icy cadence.

"Every single time you called her Illuna instead of Petal," I say.

"A minor disagreement is not a fight," Flower says defensively. "'Petal' was an infantile nickname from when we first fused. I have outgrown such childish habits."

"Adopting a severe tone doesn't make you mature, you know," I say.

"Yes, it does!" Flower insists, puffing out her cheeks. "I conduct myself with maturity, which makes me an adult. End of discussion."

Melindra tilts her head, studying her. "Flower... why do you want to be an adult so badly? Why does it matter?"

"Because I AM an adult!" Flower says. "What grown woman wants to be treated like a child?"

"Hold that thought," Melindra says.

She unslings her leather satchel, reaches deep inside, and pulls out several smooth stones, arranging them in a neat line on the dust between us.

}
"Look at these," Melindra says, pointing down at the display. "These are the pride of my collection. My absolute favorites. What do you think of them, Flower?"

"Oh, they're gorgeous!" Flower beams, her cold demeanor vanishing in an instant as she leans over them. "The banding on that grey one is incredible!"

"And you enjoy discussing the collection with me?" Melindra asks.

"I love it!" Flower says.

I lean over, squinting down at the assortment in the dim light. No matter how hard I stare, I cannot find a single feature separating them from the gravel scattered across any ditch in Varathia.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Childish, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Childish)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Childish
TEXT:

"Most people look at these stones and see gravel," Melindra says, rolling one of the rounded pebbles between her thumb and forefinger. "They dismiss collecting them as something childish."

She glances over her shoulder at me with a sly grin.

"Isn't that so, Barry?"

"To be blunt? Yes," I say, leaning back against the wall. "It's an unusual fixation. They look like ordinary dirt to me."

Flower's green eyes widen. "People think gathering stones is childish? Then why do you keep doing it?"

"Because other people's opinions are worthless," Melindra says, sitting cross-legged across from her on the rock. "It is my collection, not theirs. If they want to sneer, let them."

"Doesn't it bother you when they call you a child?" Flower asks quietly.

"A secret of adulthood," Melindra says, resting her chin in her hand, "is that you stop caring what label someone slaps on you. You are who you are. Twisting your nature to fit someone else's idea of your age is a fool's errand. If people want to call a good hobby childish, let them miss out."

Flower looks down at the smooth pebbles, silent as she turns the thought over in her hands.

"You don't have to break yourself to earn approval," Melindra adds softly. "If you like who you are, stay that way. Making yourself miserable for someone else's nod isn't worth the cost."

"It isn't about approval," Flower murmurs, her shoulders dropping. "It's... Petal is my dearest friend. I don't want to be her burden."

"Why would you think you're a burden to Illuna?" I ask.

"Because Petal is strong, and clever, and always knows what to do," Flower says, staring at her lap. "And I'm just... me. Every time we discuss my age, she looks so tired. So disappointed. I think after twenty years, she's simply exhausted by my childishness. That's why I'm trying to grow up. I don't want to fail her anymore."

"That isn't why she looks disappointed!" I blurt out. "It's because—"

I bite the words off just in time. Illuna's confession in the tunnel was raw, bleeding guilt—not something I have the right to drop on Flower uninvited.

"Listen to me," I say instead. "Illuna and I talked about this before we woke you beneath the seredium tower. She told us she has no issue with how you act. None at all. She said you are fine exactly as you are. Melindra was standing right there. Ask her."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05a-Overwhelming, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Overwhelming)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Overwhelming
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"She truly said that?" Flower looks up at Melindra, hope lighting her green eyes.

"Her exact words," Melindra says, nodding firmly.

"I... I thought she regretted being bound to me," Flower whispers, a small, tremulous smile breaking through. "She always seems so exasperated with everything I do."

"Illuna is exasperated with the entire universe," I tell her. "It has nothing to do with you. She treats everyone like an inconvenience. Rose and I tried to have a quiet word with her ten minutes ago, and the second she found an opening, she abandoned the conversation and went to sleep. Look at your lap."

Flower blinks down at the half-eaten crust. "That explains the sandwich."

"Exactly," I say. "Her bark is just habit. Don't let it shake you."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"She truly said that?" Flower looks up at Melindra, hope lighting her green eyes.

"Her exact words," Melindra says, nodding firmly.

"I... I thought she regretted being bound to me," Flower whispers, a small, tremulous smile breaking through. "She always seems so exasperated with everything I do."

"Illuna is exasperated with the entire universe," I tell her. "It has nothing to do with you. She treats everyone like an inconvenience. I tried talking about fighting together five minutes ago and she told me to hurry up or she'd conjure a book. You know how she is."

"That does sound like Petal," Flower admits with a small chuckle.

"Right," I say. "Her bark is just habit. Don't let it shake you."

}
"All right," Flower says, drawing a breath and sitting up straighter. "I'll try to stop fretting. But I need time to figure out... how I want to be. It's still tangled in my head."

"No rush," I say. "Sort it out in your own time. Just remember to fit in Arraka's drills when you have the energy. The God of Time won't drop dead on his own."

Melindra glances around the cavern. "I'm shocked Arraka hasn't chimed in. Usually she leaps at any chance to grace us with her venom."

"Arraka is asleep," Flower says, lifting the gold amulet to peer at the intricate filigree. "Her aura settles into a slow pulse when she rests."

Melindra stares. "Arraka sleeps? Since when? I've never seen her take a nap in centuries."

"She sleeps occasionally," Flower says. "A few hours a week is plenty for her. Absorbing thousands of spirits on the magical plane comes with benefits. Immense reservoirs, near-immortality, and minimal rest."

choice(""What do you mean by near-immortality?"", B3-Ch05a-Magnificence, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Magnificence, v_b3_ch5_moustache = 0)
choice(""So, how do you wake her up if you need her while she's asleep? Do you spin her amulet?"", B3-Ch05a-Magnificence, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Magnificence, v_b3_ch5_moustache = 1)
choice(""Does this mean that we can draw a fake moustache on her amulet and she won’t notice?"", B3-Ch05a-Magnificence, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05a-Magnificence, v_b3_ch5_moustache = 2)


ID: B3-Ch05a-Magnificence
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch5_moustache == 0) {
"What do you mean by near-immortality?"

"Even if you destroy her host, she doesn't die," Flower explains, turning the pendant in her small fingers. "Normal banshees perish if their possessed vessel is killed, but Arraka disperses lethal trauma across the souls she holds captive. If the vessel falls, her spirit simply drifts free to claim a new one. That's why Eiden and Eleya wove the ban barring her from humanoid vessels—to keep her contained."

"Except you," I say, "because your fusion with Illuna masks your humanity."

"Right," Flower says.

"Wait," I say, frowning into the shadows. "Why couldn't she possess Tyrath? He's a monster, and he's absurdly powerful."

"Raw strength doesn't give a banshee leverage," Flower says. "Soul resonance determines control, not the host's muscle. Even if Arraka breached Tyrath, she couldn't draw more power than an ordinary mage through his frame. Besides, dragons possess natural wards against mental subversion. The denser the spirit, the harder it is to override. Even with his guard down, she couldn't take him."

"Makes sense," I say.

"Are you drilling tonight, Flower?" Melindra asks.

"I think I'll rest," Flower says softly. "Yesterday took a lot out of us."

"Cards, then," Melindra says, dusting off her hands. "And once you tire of losing, I can show you the rest of the collection. I gathered four new specimens on my stroll."

I groan. "Not the rocks again."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_moustache == 1) {
"So, how do you wake her up if you need her while she's asleep? Do you spin her amulet?"

"Usually we just rattle the chain until she starts swearing," Flower says, giggling. "Though spinning would certainly get her attention."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_moustache == 2) {
"Does this mean that we can draw a fake moustache on her amulet and she won’t notice?"

"I actually did that once!" Flower giggles, covering her mouth. "She was furious for three days."

"Excellent," I say, pulling a charcoal stick from my pouch. "Let's see if her temper has improved."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_moustache == 1 || v_b3_ch5_moustache == 2) {
"Are you drilling tonight, Flower?" Melindra asks.

"I think I'll rest," Flower says softly. "Yesterday took a lot out of us."

"Cards, then," Melindra says, dusting off her hands. "And once you tire of losing, I can show you the rest of the collection. I gathered four new specimens on my stroll."

I groan. "Not the rocks again."

}
"Oh?" Melindra's eyes gleam with malicious delight. "Blind to the splendor of geology, are we, Barry? Then school has begun. Flower and I will spend the remainder of the night turning you into a connoisseur. By sunrise you will recite every mineral vein by heart."

"You are too generous," I say. "Truly, don't trouble yourselves."

"No trouble at all," Melindra says, pulling a fresh handful of grey stones from her satchel. "Flower, shift right—I need the light to catch the grain on this basalt."

For the next three hours, Melindra and Flower deliver an exhaustive breakdown of grain, density, luster, and fracture lines, lecturing with religious fervor on why ordinary cavern rubble constitutes high art.

By the end of the shift, my appreciation for dirt remains entirely unchanged—but at least I can tell which pebbles will provoke an academic lecture and which ones are safe to step on.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Refreshed, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Refreshed)
